WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.827
Remember the home cook from the coroutines track?

2
00:00:05.827 --> 00:00:12.399
When she needed tomatoes, she texted three shops at once, and waited only as long as the slowest reply.

3
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.040
Now she works the post office counter.

4
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.701
A customer asks what it costs to send a parcel to Lisbon.

5
00:00:18.701 --> 00:00:24.481
She texts three couriers, Northline, Eastgate and Fjord, and passes on the cheapest reply.

6
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:31.597
Say Northline needs 120 milliseconds to answer, Eastgate 300, and Fjord 200.

7
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:39.947
The first version anyone writes asks one courier, waits for the answer, then asks the next.

8
00:00:39.947 --> 00:00:41.175
Northline, 120.

9
00:00:41.175 --> 00:00:43.139
Then Eastgate, 300 more.

10
00:00:43.139 --> 00:00:44.857
Then Fjord, 200 more.

11
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:51.234
620 milliseconds for three numbers, and most of that time the cook is just staring at her phone.

12
00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:54.189
Meanwhile the customer is staring at the cook.

13
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.164
What we'd like is obvious.

14
00:00:56.164 --> 00:00:59.149
Send all three texts, then read the replies.

15
00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:09.125
The tool is the coroutine: a piece of work that can pause without holding its thread, like a dish the cook leaves to simmer while she starts the next one.

16
00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:10.774
Let's map the texting.

17
00:01:10.774 --> 00:01:12.345
Sending a text is async.

18
00:01:12.345 --> 00:01:18.173
It starts a coroutine for one courier and hands back a ticket at once, called a Deferred.

19
00:01:18.173 --> 00:01:20.268
Reading the replies is awaitAll.

20
00:01:20.268 --> 00:01:23.084
It waits until every ticket has its answer.

21
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.526
So all three texts go out together.

22
00:01:25.526 --> 00:01:28.071
How long until she has all three prices?

23
00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:33.304
Northline replies at 120.

24
00:01:33.304 --> 00:01:34.467
Fjord at 200.

25
00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:35.899
Eastgate at 300.

26
00:01:35.899 --> 00:01:39.389
She has everything at 300 milliseconds.

27
00:01:39.389 --> 00:01:42.343
The slowest decides, not the sum.

28
00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:52.643
The lesson's demo prints exactly that on a fake clock: 620 milliseconds one after another, 300 all at once, and the same three prices.

29
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:59.560
In Kotlin, that's one line inside coroutineScope: text all three, then read the replies.

30
00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:06.916
coroutineScope is the parent that waits for every call it started, so none of them can outlive the function.

31
00:02:06.916 --> 00:02:08.754
The Java twin uses Reactor.

32
00:02:08.754 --> 00:02:13.113
Mono.zip asks all three at once and waits for a value from each.

33
00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:15.316
That was a good day.

34
00:02:15.316 --> 00:02:19.932
But a customer standing at a counter won't wait two seconds for a price.

35
00:02:19.932 --> 00:02:23.201
So the cook sets a kitchen timer: 250 milliseconds.

36
00:02:23.201 --> 00:02:29.484
That's the deadline, the moment by which the request must answer, whatever the couriers are doing.

37
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:34.638
And here's the question people are shy to ask, because it sounds too simple.

38
00:02:34.638 --> 00:02:36.198
Where does the timer go?

39
00:02:36.198 --> 00:02:38.212
One timer around the whole job?

40
00:02:38.212 --> 00:02:39.967
Or one timer for each text?

41
00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:45.465
Let's try the obvious one first.

42
00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:47.475
One timer around everything.

43
00:02:47.475 --> 00:02:49.558
At 250 milliseconds it rings.

44
00:02:49.558 --> 00:02:51.353
Eastgate is still typing.

45
00:02:51.353 --> 00:02:53.867
So the timer cancels the whole job.

46
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:58.579
And the whole job includes the replies that already came in.

47
00:02:58.579 --> 00:03:03.507
Northline answered at 120, Fjord at 200, and both get thrown away.

48
00:03:03.507 --> 00:03:05.448
The customer gets nothing.

49
00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:05.996
Well.

50
00:03:05.996 --> 00:03:10.537
That's two good prices in the bin, because a third shop types slowly.

51
00:03:10.537 --> 00:03:14.881
So the point is: the timer should cancel only the one who is late.

52
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.364
Now one timer per text.

53
00:03:17.364 --> 00:03:22.428
At 250, Eastgate's timer rings, and only Eastgate's call is cancelled.

54
00:03:22.428 --> 00:03:25.828
Its answer becomes null, which means no answer.

55
00:03:25.828 --> 00:03:28.143
Northline and Fjord still count.

56
00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:34.263
The demo shows it: 250 milliseconds, Northline 12.45, null, Fjord 12.43.

57
00:03:34.263 --> 00:03:37.620
The customer gets Fjord's 12.43, on time.

58
00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:45.645
Eastgate's 12.41 would have been cheaper, but a price that misses the deadline is no price at all.

59
00:03:45.867 --> 00:03:53.121
And because all three texts start together, a timer per text is also a timer for the whole request.

60
00:03:53.121 --> 00:03:54.733
Nobody waits past 250.

61
00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:59.455
The Kotlin word for a timer per call is withTimeoutOrNull.

62
00:03:59.455 --> 00:04:01.219
It's a cancel with a timer.

63
00:04:01.219 --> 00:04:05.791
At the deadline, it cancels the late call and gives back null instead.

64
00:04:05.791 --> 00:04:10.755
Java's Reactor does the same with a timeout that turns into an empty answer.

65
00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:14.228
Here the kitchen picture bends a little.

66
00:04:14.228 --> 00:04:17.813
A shop that gets a never-mind text stops typing at once.

67
00:04:17.813 --> 00:04:22.806
A cancelled coroutine stops only at its next pause, its next suspension point.

68
00:04:22.806 --> 00:04:27.480
A courier call stuck in a blocking wait keeps going until that wait ends.

69
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.696
Let's test the rule at its edge.

70
00:04:30.696 --> 00:04:32.991
Make the timer 100 milliseconds.

71
00:04:32.991 --> 00:04:34.426
Nobody is that fast.

72
00:04:34.426 --> 00:04:36.363
What does the customer get?

73
00:04:39.133 --> 00:04:41.275
At 100 milliseconds, three nulls.

74
00:04:41.275 --> 00:04:43.221
That's not a bug in the timer.

75
00:04:43.221 --> 00:04:45.881
It's a real day: nobody answered in time.

76
00:04:45.881 --> 00:04:53.603
Later in the lesson, the service serves the last good price, marked stale, so the customer still gets something honest.

77
00:04:54.533 --> 00:04:56.873
And a bad day has one more surprise.

78
00:04:56.873 --> 00:05:00.512
A courier doesn't just run late, it fails with an error.

79
00:05:00.512 --> 00:05:04.216
Inside coroutineScope, one failure cancels the other two.

80
00:05:04.216 --> 00:05:06.165
That's the next section's fix.

81
00:05:07.067 --> 00:05:08.890
So, where does the timer go?

82
00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:11.040
On each call, not around the job.

83
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:19.247
Text everyone at once, let the slowest decide, and let a late courier drop out alone, without taking the good answers with it.

84
00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:20.242
Quick check.

85
00:05:20.242 --> 00:05:25.926
One timer around the whole job, and this time all three couriers answer before it rings.

86
00:05:25.926 --> 00:05:28.575
Does the one-timer version lose anything?

87
00:05:31.767 --> 00:05:31.966
No.

88
00:05:31.966 --> 00:05:34.818
Nothing was late, so nothing was cancelled.

89
00:05:34.818 --> 00:05:40.789
One timer only throws answers away when someone is late, and that's the day you needed it.
